      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-178",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Subfolder deny lists compare path segments case-sensitively, so a paired device can read and write host-denied folders on case-insensitive filesystems.\n\nThe client-supplied path (`/files/<path>`, upload `path=`, file-ops `path=`/`to=`, zip `path=`) is lexically normalized, and its first segment is checked with an exact, case-sensitive `TreeSet.contains` against deny-list entries copied from disk with their exact case (ServerWindow.showSubfoldersDialog). The same path is then handed to java.nio Files APIs, which resolve it under the filesystem's own rules. The project targets Windows and macOS (README.md:12), whose default filesystems (NTFS, APFS) are case-insensitive, so `PRIVATE` passes the check and still opens `Private`. This follows from the code plus documented platform behaviour; I did not run it. Windows' trailing-dot stripping (`Private.`), 8.3 short names (`PRIVAT~1`) and macOS NFC/NFD Unicode equivalence are the same class of weakness, but I am less sure of those variants.\n\nImpact: The host-configured per-subfolder read and write restrictions are fully bypassed: the device can read, zip-download, upload into, rename, move and trash files in folders the host explicitly denied. Access stays inside the device's own subtree.\n\nExploit scenario: The host blocks device 'kid' from reading or writing the subfolder 'Private' in its tree. From the kid device the attacker requests GET /files/PRIVATE/taxes.pdf. firstSegment returns 'PRIVATE', which is not in denyRead={'Private'}, so the check passes, and NTFS/APFS opens Private/taxes.pdf and streams it. GET /api/zip?path=PRIVATE downloads the whole folder the same way. POST /api/upload/init?path=PRIVATE/x.txt (topFolder 'PRIVATE') followed by chunk/complete writes a file into the write-denied folder. POST /api/files/delete?path=PRIVATE/taxes.pdf or move?path=PRIVATE/...&to=public moves files out of it.\n\nPreconditions: Host runs localReceiver on a case-insensitive filesystem (Windows NTFS or macOS APFS/HFS+ default); Pairing mode, with the host having unticked Read or Write for a subfolder in the device's subfolder dialog (deny list configured); Attacker holds a paired device session (or a stolen session cookie) for that device.\n\nRecommendation: Do not authorize by comparing raw strings. Resolve the target, and each deny-listed folder under the device root, to a canonical form (for example toRealPath() on the deepest existing ancestor, or Files.isSameFile against the denied folder) and compare those. At minimum, compare case-insensitively and NFC-normalized on case-insensitive platforms, and reject segments with trailing dots or spaces, or containing '~', before the deny-list check. Apply the same fix in canWriteSub (line 75) and UploadHandler.topFolder.\n\nCWE-178. Severity MEDIUM, confidence high."
          },
          "locations": [
