        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-552",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "GET /files/ does not exclude the internal staging and trash directories, exposing other devices' in-flight uploads and trashed files.\n\nThe client path in /files/<path> is only checked to stay under the device root. FileOpsHandler.resolveSafe (lines 157-159) and ZipHandler (lines 58-59) also reject targets under fileRoot/.localreceiver-part and fileRoot/.localreceiver-trash, but FilesHandler does not; the PART_DIR/TRASH filter at lines 146-147 only hides those names from listings. A device whose root is the whole file root can therefore list and download the shared staging and recycle-bin areas directly, even though TrashHandler documents that trash items are visible only to the deleting device.\n\nImpact: Confidentiality: other devices' deleted files and partially uploaded files can be read, and the per-subfolder read deny list is bypassed for content that passes through staging or the trash. Device ids and transfer keys are also disclosed.\n\nExploit scenario: A device scoped to the whole root, with 'Private' deny-listed for reading, sends GET /files/.localreceiver-trash/ to list every trash item id from all devices, then GET /files/.localreceiver-trash/<id>/meta.properties and /files/.localreceiver-trash/<id>/item/<name> to download files another device deleted from 'Private' or from its own folder. GET /files/.localreceiver-part/ enumerates every device id and upload key (`<deviceId>-<key>`), and /files/.localreceiver-part/<id>-<key>/N.chunk returns the raw chunks of other devices' in-progress uploads, including uploads bound for folders this device may not read.\n\nPreconditions: Pairing mode with a device whose folder the host has set to the whole shared root (relPath \"\"), which is not the default; For the deny-list bypass, the host has deny-listed some subfolders for that device.\n\nRecommendation: In FilesHandler.handle, reject (403/404) any target that starts with fileRoot.resolve(UploadHandler.PART_DIR) or fileRoot.resolve(TrashHandler.DIR), matching FileOpsHandler.resolveSafe. Better, put one shared resolve-and-confine helper in front of every handler. Also make FileOpsHandler.rename reject the reserved names (use the same check sanitizePath applies), so a device cannot create these directories by renaming.\n\nCWE-552. Severity MEDIUM, confidence high."
          },
