        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-863",
          "ruleIndex": 2,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "A read-denied subfolder can be renamed or moved to a new name, which escapes the name-keyed read deny list.\n\nFile operations from a paired device are authorized only with canWriteSub on the source/destination's first segment. The read deny list is keyed by folder name, so a device that may write to a read-denied top-level folder can rename it (line 186) or move it (line 145). The data then sits under a name the deny list does not cover, and /files/ and /api/zip serve it.\n\nImpact: Full read access to a folder the host explicitly marked unreadable for that device, plus the deny protection is removed for every other device restricted by the same folder name.\n\nExploit scenario: The host hides the folder `private` from device A (relPath \"\") by unticking only its Read box, which is the natural way to hide a folder. Write stays allowed by default. A sends `POST /api/files/rename?path=private&to=loot`. resolveSafe accepts it, canWriteSub(\"private\") is true and the folder is renamed. A then sends `GET /api/zip?path=loot` (canReadSub(\"loot\") is true) and downloads everything. `POST /api/files/move?path=private&to=<some readable folder>` gives the same result. The rename also strips the deny entry from every other device that had `private` denied.\n\nPreconditions: Attacker holds a paired device token with the device-level Write grant; The host denied Read but not Write on a top-level subfolder (the GUI allows this; write is allowed by default).\n\nRecommendation: Treat moving, renaming or deleting an entry as requiring read access too: check canReadSub on the source in rename/move/delete. Refuse to rename or move any top-level folder that appears in denyRead or denyWrite. Alternatively, make denyRead imply denyWrite, or bind deny entries to something the device cannot rename (e.g. a stable folder identity kept server-side).\n\nCWE-863. Severity MEDIUM, confidence high."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/main/java/localreceiver/server/FileOpsHandler.java",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 186,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "Files.move(target, destination);"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "rename",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "rename"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "claudeSecurityPlugin": {
              "id": "F3",
              "title": "A read-denied subfolder can be renamed or moved to a new name, which escapes the name-keyed read deny list",
              "impact": "Full read access to a folder the host explicitly marked unreadable for that device, plus the deny protection is removed for every other device restricted by the same folder name.",
              "file": "src/main/java/localreceiver/server/FileOpsHandler.java",
              "line": 186,
              "description": "File operations from a paired device are authorized only with canWriteSub on the source/destination's first segment. The read deny list is keyed by folder name, so a device that may write to a read-denied top-level folder can rename it (line 186) or move it (line 145). The data then sits under a name the deny list does not cover, and /files/ and /api/zip serve it.",
              "exploit_scenario": "The host hides the folder `private` from device A (relPath \"\") by unticking only its Read box, which is the natural way to hide a folder. Write stays allowed by default. A sends `POST /api/files/rename?path=private&to=loot`. resolveSafe accepts it, canWriteSub(\"private\") is true and the folder is renamed. A then sends `GET /api/zip?path=loot` (canReadSub(\"loot\") is true) and downloads everything. `POST /api/files/move?path=private&to=<some readable folder>` gives the same result. The rename also strips the deny entry from every other device that had `private` denied.",
              "preconditions": [
                "Attacker holds a paired device token with the device-level Write grant",
                "The host denied Read but not Write on a top-level subfolder (the GUI allows this; write is allowed by default)"
              ],
              "category": "Incorrect Authorization",
              "severity": "MEDIUM",
              "confidence": "high",
              "recommendation": "Treat moving, renaming or deleting an entry as requiring read access too: check canReadSub on the source in rename/move/delete. Refuse to rename or move any top-level folder that appears in denyRead or denyWrite. Alternatively, make denyRead imply denyWrite, or bind deny entries to something the device cannot rename (e.g. a stable folder identity kept server-side).",
              "cwe_id": "CWE-863",
              "snippet": "Files.move(target, destination);",
              "symbol": "rename",
