        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-863",
          "ruleIndex": 2,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "/files/ serves the shared trash and upload staging folders, exposing other devices' deleted files and in-flight uploads and bypassing deny lists.\n\nThe client-supplied /files/ path is checked only for confinement to the device root and for the first-segment deny list. Unlike FileOpsHandler and ZipHandler, it does not exclude `.localreceiver-trash` or `.localreceiver-part`. All devices' deleted items and upload chunks are stored under the file root, so a device scoped to the whole root can list and download them directly. The TrashHandler ownership check (device id in meta.properties) and the deny lists never run on this path.\n\nImpact: Confidentiality breach of other devices' deleted files and partial uploads, including content from folders the host denied to the reader. This breaks the documented per-device recycle-bin isolation.\n\nExploit scenario: Device A has been widened to the whole shared folder (relPath \"\") but has denyRead {\"bob\"}. Device B is scoped to `bob`. B deletes bob/tax.pdf, which moves it to <root>/.localreceiver-trash/1759650000000-3fa2/item/tax.pdf. A sends `GET /files/.localreceiver-trash/` with Accept: application/json and gets every trash item id. It reads `/files/.localreceiver-trash/<id>/meta.properties` (origPath, deleting device id) and downloads `/files/.localreceiver-trash/<id>/item/tax.pdf`. In the same way, `GET /files/.localreceiver-part/` and `/files/.localreceiver-part/<Bid>-<key>/<n>.chunk` expose B's in-progress or abandoned uploads into the denied folder.\n\nPreconditions: Attacker holds a paired device token whose folder is the whole shared root (relPath \"\"), granted by the host; Another device has deleted files or has staged uploads, typically from a folder the attacker is denied.\n\nRecommendation: In FilesHandler.handle, reject (403/404) any target that starts with fileRoot.resolve(UploadHandler.PART_DIR) or fileRoot.resolve(TrashHandler.DIR), matching resolveSafe in FileOpsHandler. Better, move staging and trash outside the served file root.\n\nCWE-863. Severity MEDIUM, confidence high."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/main/java/localreceiver/server/FilesHandler.java",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 102,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "if (!device.canReadSub(Devices.Device.firstSegment(root, target))) {"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "handle",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "handle"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "claudeSecurityPlugin": {
              "id": "F4",
              "title": "/files/ serves the shared trash and upload staging folders, exposing other devices' deleted files and in-flight uploads and bypassing deny lists",
              "impact": "Confidentiality breach of other devices' deleted files and partial uploads, including content from folders the host denied to the reader. This breaks the documented per-device recycle-bin isolation.",
              "file": "src/main/java/localreceiver/server/FilesHandler.java",
              "line": 102,
              "description": "The client-supplied /files/ path is checked only for confinement to the device root and for the first-segment deny list. Unlike FileOpsHandler and ZipHandler, it does not exclude `.localreceiver-trash` or `.localreceiver-part`. All devices' deleted items and upload chunks are stored under the file root, so a device scoped to the whole root can list and download them directly. The TrashHandler ownership check (device id in meta.properties) and the deny lists never run on this path.",
              "exploit_scenario": "Device A has been widened to the whole shared folder (relPath \"\") but has denyRead {\"bob\"}. Device B is scoped to `bob`. B deletes bob/tax.pdf, which moves it to <root>/.localreceiver-trash/1759650000000-3fa2/item/tax.pdf. A sends `GET /files/.localreceiver-trash/` with Accept: application/json and gets every trash item id. It reads `/files/.localreceiver-trash/<id>/meta.properties` (origPath, deleting device id) and downloads `/files/.localreceiver-trash/<id>/item/tax.pdf`. In the same way, `GET /files/.localreceiver-part/` and `/files/.localreceiver-part/<Bid>-<key>/<n>.chunk` expose B's in-progress or abandoned uploads into the denied folder.",
              "preconditions": [
                "Attacker holds a paired device token whose folder is the whole shared root (relPath \"\"), granted by the host",
                "Another device has deleted files or has staged uploads, typically from a folder the attacker is denied"
              ],
              "category": "Incorrect Authorization",
              "severity": "MEDIUM",
              "confidence": "high",
              "recommendation": "In FilesHandler.handle, reject (403/404) any target that starts with fileRoot.resolve(UploadHandler.PART_DIR) or fileRoot.resolve(TrashHandler.DIR), matching resolveSafe in FileOpsHandler. Better, move staging and trash outside the served file root.",
              "cwe_id": "CWE-863",
              "snippet": "if (!device.canReadSub(Devices.Device.firstSegment(root, target))) {",
              "symbol": "handle",
              "declared_line": 102,
              "via_change": null,
              "other_cwe_ids": [],
              "claudeSecurityPluginFindingId": "v3:f2f538845fae529293fcee8e923198386659229c0fe0f991dc5069c5210d1d01",
