        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-345",
          "ruleIndex": 3,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "CA fingerprint shown to the user is a separate server response, not the fingerprint of the certificate the device installs, so the out-of-band check does not stop CA substitution.\n\ncert-check.js gets the 'Certificate SHA-256' it shows from its own GET /ca-fingerprint request, over the same untrusted channel. It never looks at the bytes of /ca.crt. index.html and cert-help.html tell the user to install the certificate if this value matches the host window. An on-path attacker who swaps the /ca.crt body and passes /ca-fingerprint through unchanged makes the page and the window match, so the user installs the attacker's root CA.\n\nImpact: The attacker's root CA is installed permanently in the victim device's trust store. The attacker can then issue trusted certificates for any hostname and intercept or modify all of that device's HTTPS traffic (banking, email, localReceiver session cookies) wherever they hold an on-path position. Even the real localReceiver CA is unconstrained (TlsSupport.generateCa adds no name-constraints extension), so the 'can only vouch for that computer's own localReceiver servers' claim in cert-help.html:26-27 does not hold either. This is an incomplete fix for the 'CA substitution during first pairing' issue that commit 19d0aec set out to address.\n\nExploit scenario: The attacker sits on the network path between a phone and the localReceiver host. When the phone opens https://<host>:<port>/, the attacker ends TLS with their own certificate. The phone does not trust the localReceiver CA yet, so it warns exactly as it would for the real server, and the user proceeds. The attacker forwards every request to the real server unchanged except GET /ca.crt, for which it returns its own root CA certificate. GET /ca-fingerprint (and the X-CA-Fingerprint-SHA256 header) pass through untouched, so cert-check.js shows the real CA fingerprint. The user compares it with the fingerprint in the host window, sees a match, and installs and fully trusts the attacker's certificate as a root CA. The page's 'If the two differ, do not install it' check passes because it compares two copies of the real fingerprint and never the certificate being installed. In --http mode (non-default) neither the page (cert-check.js:7) nor the host window (ServerWindow.java:517) shows a fingerprint, so there is no check at all.\n\nPreconditions: Attacker has an on-path position on the LAN (e.g. ARP or DHCP spoofing on the same Wi-Fi) while the victim device installs the certificate for the first time; The victim device has not yet trusted the localReceiver CA, so the browser shows the same untrusted-certificate warning for the real server and for the attacker's TLS endpoint, and the user clicks through; The victim follows the app's own install guidance (index.html footer link / cert-help.html).\n\nRecommendation: Do not treat any value rendered by the web page as the verification source. Under MITM the attacker controls the page, cert-check.js and every response, so hashing /ca.crt in the browser would not help either. Tell users to compare the host window's SHA-256 fingerprint with the one shown by the OS certificate installer or trust-store UI (iOS profile 'More Details', Android certificate details, Windows certificate dialog Details tab, macOS Keychain), which comes from the certificate bytes actually being installed. Remove or clearly caveat the page-displayed fingerprint and the X-CA-Fingerprint-SHA256 header so they are not presented as a security check. Separately, add a name-constraints extension to the CA (or stop claiming it is scoped) and do not offer CA installation in HTTP mode without an out-of-band fingerprint.\n\nCWE-345. Severity MEDIUM, confidence high."
          },
