        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-863",
          "ruleIndex": 2,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Pairing toggle updates the old server object after a root change, so the running server stays in open mode while the GUI shows pairing as required.\n\nThe pairingBox listener is a lambda inside the constructor, so `server` resolves to the constructor parameter, which shadows the field. chooseRoot() later sets the field to a new LocalReceiverServer at line 377, but every later toggle still calls setPairingRequired on the original, stopped instance. The live server's authorize() decision at Devices.java:141 keeps whatever pairing value it had when the root changed.\n\nImpact: Pairing is silently disabled for the rest of the session while the UI reports it as enforced. Every unauthenticated LAN client gets full read and write access to the shared root, and the per-device folder scopes and deny lists are bypassed because the OPEN device is scoped to everything.\n\nExploit scenario: The operator last ran localReceiver in open mode, so pairing=false is persisted. At launch they click 'Change…' and pick a different shared folder. chooseRoot builds a new server with pairingRequired=false. The operator then ticks 'Require device pairing' and clicks Start. The tick only reaches the discarded server. startOn() mints a pairing code on the new server and the window shows a normal 'Pair:' link with a QR code, so the GUI looks fully locked down. In fact the running server returns Devices.OPEN for every request. Any host on the LAN can skip pairing and list, download, upload, rename, move and delete files across the whole shared root through /files/ and /api/*.\n\nPreconditions: The operator has 'Require device pairing' unticked (not the default; persisted via config or --open) when they use 'Change…' to pick a new file root in the same session; The operator then ticks 'Require device pairing' and starts or keeps running the server; The attacker can reach the server's port on the LAN.\n\nRecommendation: Do not let constructor lambdas capture the shadowing parameter. Reference the field explicitly (this.server.setPairingRequired(...), this.server.isRunning(), this.server.devices()) in the dirBrowseBox and pairingBox listeners. Or rename the constructor parameter so the field is the only `server` in scope. Consider asserting after each toggle that server.isPairingRequired() == pairingBox.isSelected().\n\nCWE-863. Severity MEDIUM, confidence high."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/main/java/localreceiver/gui/ServerWindow.java",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 140,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "            server.setPairingRequired(pairingBox.isSelected());"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "ServerWindow.ServerWindow (pairingBox action listener)",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "ServerWindow.ServerWindow (pairingBox action listener)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
