        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-352",
          "ruleIndex": 4,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Open mode authorizes every request, including cross-site ones, with full read/write and no Origin/Host check (CSRF, and DNS rebinding over HTTP).\n\nWith pairing off, authorize() returns the full-access OPEN device for any request. No handler validates Origin, Referer or Host, or uses an anti-CSRF token. Writes are plain POSTs with query-string parameters and no body, so any web page a LAN user visits can trigger them. Under --http, a DNS-rebinding page can also read every file, because the Host header is never checked.\n\nImpact: An internet attacker, not just the trusted LAN, can delete, rename or move files in the share. In HTTP+open mode they can also exfiltrate and overwrite the entire shared folder.\n\nExploit scenario: The host runs `--open` (or turns pairing off in the GUI). A LAN user visits an attacker's page, which auto-submits `<form method=POST action=\"https://192.168.1.10:4646/api/files/delete?path=Photos\">`. The default port 4646 and common LAN ranges make the target guessable. Over HTTPS this works once the browser trusts the app's CA, which the app tells users to install. The folder is moved to the trash, and similar forms rename/move/mkdir arbitrary guessable paths. If the server also runs `--http`, the page can DNS-rebind its own hostname to the LAN IP and, being same-origin, read `GET /api/zip` (the whole share) and use the PUT chunk endpoint to upload files. The PUT route is otherwise blocked by CORS preflight.\n\nPreconditions: Pairing disabled (`--open` / GUI toggle; non-default); A user on the LAN visits an attacker-controlled page; For CSRF over HTTPS, the victim browser trusts the localReceiver CA; for DNS-rebinding reads, the server runs `--http`; Browser does not block public-to-private requests (e.g. Chrome Local Network Access prompt not granted).\n\nRecommendation: Reject state-changing requests whose Origin (or Referer) is not the server's own origin, and reject requests whose Host header is not a known LAN IP/hostname of the server (stops DNS rebinding). Also, or alternatively, require a custom header (e.g. X-LocalReceiver: 1) on all API calls so cross-site requests trigger a CORS preflight, and keep this protection active in open mode.\n\nCWE-352. Severity MEDIUM, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/main/java/localreceiver/server/Devices.java",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 142,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "return OPEN;"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "authorize",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "authorize"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "claudeSecurityPlugin": {
              "id": "F7",
              "title": "Open mode authorizes every request, including cross-site ones, with full read/write and no Origin/Host check (CSRF, and DNS rebinding over HTTP)",
              "impact": "An internet attacker, not just the trusted LAN, can delete, rename or move files in the share. In HTTP+open mode they can also exfiltrate and overwrite the entire shared folder.",
              "file": "src/main/java/localreceiver/server/Devices.java",
              "line": 142,
              "description": "With pairing off, authorize() returns the full-access OPEN device for any request. No handler validates Origin, Referer or Host, or uses an anti-CSRF token. Writes are plain POSTs with query-string parameters and no body, so any web page a LAN user visits can trigger them. Under --http, a DNS-rebinding page can also read every file, because the Host header is never checked.",
              "exploit_scenario": "The host runs `--open` (or turns pairing off in the GUI). A LAN user visits an attacker's page, which auto-submits `<form method=POST action=\"https://192.168.1.10:4646/api/files/delete?path=Photos\">`. The default port 4646 and common LAN ranges make the target guessable. Over HTTPS this works once the browser trusts the app's CA, which the app tells users to install. The folder is moved to the trash, and similar forms rename/move/mkdir arbitrary guessable paths. If the server also runs `--http`, the page can DNS-rebind its own hostname to the LAN IP and, being same-origin, read `GET /api/zip` (the whole share) and use the PUT chunk endpoint to upload files. The PUT route is otherwise blocked by CORS preflight.",
              "preconditions": [
                "Pairing disabled (`--open` / GUI toggle; non-default)",
                "A user on the LAN visits an attacker-controlled page",
