        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-552",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "TLS CA private key, keystore password and device token hashes can be downloaded through /files/ and /api/zip when the shared folder contains the config directory.\n\nThe TLS secrets live in Config.dir() (default ~/.config/localreceiver: ca.p12, keystore.p12, keystore.pass, devices.properties). The shared folder defaults to user.dir, and nothing in the server stops it from containing that config directory. FilesHandler checks only that the path stays inside the device root. It hides only .localreceiver-part and .localreceiver-trash, and only in listings. So GET /files/.config/localreceiver/keystore.pass and ca.p12 reach the file read at this line. ZipHandler (lines 77-91) skips only the same two folders, so one GET /api/zip with no path packs the whole config directory.\n\nImpact: The CA private key and the server TLS key are exposed. The CA is trusted with no name restrictions on every device that installed it, so the attacker can intercept those devices' HTTPS traffic to any site, beyond localReceiver itself. Device session-token hashes and permissions in devices.properties are also exposed.\n\nExploit scenario: The host starts localReceiver from their home directory and turns pairing off (or gives one device whole-folder access). A device on the LAN requests GET /files/.config/localreceiver/keystore.pass and GET /files/.config/localreceiver/ca.p12, or a single GET /api/zip. It opens ca.p12 with the password and gets the localReceiver CA private key. The user installed this CA as a trusted root on every paired phone and laptop, as the app encourages. The CA has no name constraints and is valid for 10 years, so the attacker can issue certificates for any domain and intercept those devices' HTTPS traffic to any site. The fingerprint check does not help, because the stolen CA is the genuine one. The attacker also gets keystore.p12 (the server's own key), so they can impersonate the localReceiver server. With write access they could also move away or replace devices.properties, the stored session-token hashes.\n\nPreconditions: The shared folder contains the config directory, e.g. the server is started from $HOME (the default root is the working directory), or the host passes --root ~ or a parent of it. The README tells users to put the jar in the folder they want to share; a desktop double-click launch that starts in the home directory is plausible but was not verified here.; The requester can read the whole shared folder: the server is in --open mode (pairing off, so no authentication at all), or the host widened a paired device's folder to \"\" (whole shared folder). A newly paired device is limited to its own subfolder by default and cannot reach the config directory.; HTTPS mode was run at least once, so ca.p12 and keystore.pass exist.\n\nRecommendation: Refuse to serve anything inside Config.dir(). Block paths whose real path (toRealPath) is inside the config directory in FilesHandler, ZipHandler, FileOpsHandler and UploadHandler. Refuse to start, or warn, when the file root contains the config directory. Also consider denying dot-directories by default. Keep the CA key in a non-exportable OS keystore where possible, or give the CA name constraints. Note that keytool cannot add name constraints, so this would mean generating the CA another way.\n\nCWE-552. Severity MEDIUM, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/main/java/localreceiver/server/FilesHandler.java",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
