        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-203",
          "ruleIndex": 5,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unauthenticated /api/pair reveals which device names are paired (409 'taken' before the code is checked).\n\nPOST /api/pair is reachable without a session. Devices.pair() checks whether the client-supplied name is taken before it validates the pairing code. Anyone can therefore send any code with a candidate name and tell from the 409 'taken' vs 403 'code' response whether a device with that name exists. New devices' folder names are seeded from their names.\n\nImpact: Pre-authentication disclosure of the paired device names (and the default per-device folder names), which helps with reconnaissance and social engineering. It does not by itself grant access.\n\nExploit scenario: An unpaired LAN client sends `POST /api/pair?code=AAAA-AAAA&name=alice`, `...&name=dad_phone`, and so on. A 409 response confirms that the device and, by default, its folder name exist. A 403 means the name is not in use. No code is consumed and there is no rate limit.\n\nPreconditions: Network reach to the server port; Pairing mode (default).\n\nRecommendation: Validate the pairing code first, without consuming it. Only check the name for a valid code, then consume the code. This keeps the 'a rejected name does not burn the code' behaviour while hiding name existence from callers without a code.\n\nCWE-204. Severity LOW, confidence medium."
          },
